# HBC PBMC workflow: executable specification

This notebook is the source of truth for the planned BioLang book. It uses the
same public control and interferon-stimulated PBMC count matrices and the same
QC thresholds as the HBC training workflow. The implementation is BioLang's
MIT-licensed, paper-derived code. Seurat is not loaded, linked, invoked, or
required by this notebook.

Run from the BioLang repository root after placing `ctrl_raw/` and `stim_raw/`
there:

```biolang
# @skip
# target/release/bl notebook packages/singlecell/examples/hbc_course_validation.bln
```

The original course is at
<https://hbctraining.github.io/Intro-to-scRNAseq/>. Its reported results are
reference checkpoints, not code dependencies.

## 1. Load the two count matrices

```biolang
import "singlecell" as sc

let ctrl_raw = sc.load("ctrl_raw")
let stim_raw = sc.load("stim_raw")
println("raw ctrl: " + str(ctrl_raw.n_cells) + " barcodes x " + str(ctrl_raw.n_genes) + " genes")
println("raw stim: " + str(stim_raw.n_cells) + " barcodes x " + str(stim_raw.n_genes) + " genes")
```

The raw matrices include the full droplet barcode space. HBC first creates
objects with at least 100 detected genes; the stricter filter below includes
that condition, so applying it directly is equivalent for retained cells.

## 2. Apply the published HBC cell filters

The retained cell must have at least 500 UMIs, at least 250 detected genes,
novelty above 0.80, and mitochondrial fraction below 20 percent.

```biolang
fn hbc_filter(obj) {
    let metrics = cell_qc(obj.matrix, obj.genes)
    let genes = col(metrics, "n_genes")
    let umis = col(metrics, "total_counts")
    let mito_pct = col(metrics, "pct_mito")
    let keep = range(0, obj.n_cells) |> filter(|i| {
        umis[i] >= 500.0 and
        genes[i] >= 250.0 and
        (log10(genes[i]) / log10(umis[i])) > 0.80 and
        mito_pct[i] < 20.0
    })
    sc_subset_cells(obj, keep)
}

let ctrl = hbc_filter(ctrl_raw)
let stim = hbc_filter(stim_raw)
println("filtered ctrl: " + str(ctrl.n_cells) + " cells (HBC: 14847)")
println("filtered stim: " + str(stim.n_cells) + " cells (HBC: 14782)")
```

## 3. Merge and retain genes detected in ten cells

```biolang
let merged_cells = sc.merge(ctrl, stim, "ctrl", "stim")
let merged_gene_qc = gene_qc(merged_cells.matrix, merged_cells.genes)
let detected_cells = col(merged_gene_qc, "n_cells")
let retained_gene_indices = range(0, merged_cells.n_genes)
    |> filter(|gene| detected_cells[gene] >= 10)
let ctrl_filtered = sc_subset_genes(ctrl, retained_gene_indices)
let stim_filtered = sc_subset_genes(stim, retained_gene_indices)
let filtered = sc.merge(ctrl_filtered, stim_filtered, "ctrl", "stim")
println("merged: " + str(filtered.n_cells) + " cells (HBC: 29629)")
println("genes:  " + str(filtered.n_genes) + " genes (HBC: 14065)")

if filtered.n_cells != 29629 {
    error("HBC cell checkpoint failed: expected 29629, got " + str(filtered.n_cells))
}
if filtered.n_genes != 14065 {
    error("HBC gene checkpoint failed: expected 14065, got " + str(filtered.n_genes))
}
```

## 4. Normalize and select 3,000 variable genes

The samples are transformed separately with the paper-derived SCT v2 offset
model. Mitochondrial ratio is removed in the documented second linear model.
Features that vary consistently across samples are matched with mutual-neighbor
anchors in a scalable, sketched CCA subspace. HBC leaves anchor dimensions at
Seurat's 30-dimension default, then uses 40 integrated PCs downstream. This numerical approximation
is validated by cell-neighborhood, clustering, and marker agreement—not by
requiring identical floating-point coordinates.

```biolang
let ctrl_mito = col(cell_qc(ctrl.matrix, ctrl.genes), "pct_mito")
let stim_mito = col(cell_qc(stim.matrix, stim.genes), "pct_mito")
println("SCTransform v2: fitting the control sample")
let ctrl_sct = sc.sctransform(ctrl_filtered, 5000, ctrl_mito)
println("SCTransform v2: fitting the stimulated sample")
let stim_sct = sc.sctransform(stim_filtered, 5000, stim_mito)
let integration_features = sc.select_integration_features(ctrl_sct, stim_sct, 3000)
println("selected integration features: " + str(len(integration_features)))
write_csv(table(range(0, len(integration_features)) |> map(|i| {
    {rank: i + 1, gene: ctrl_filtered.genes[integration_features[i]]}
})), "validation-results/hbc-biolang/features.csv")

println("CCA: finding cross-sample anchors")
let anchors = sc.find_integration_anchors(ctrl_sct, stim_sct,
    "cca", 3000, 30, 5, 30)
println("anchors: " + str(len(anchors.anchors)))
println("CCA compute backend: " + anchors.compute_backend)
println("IntegrateData: applying local anchor corrections")
let corrected = sc.integrate_data(anchors, 100, 1.0)
```

## 5. SNN graph and Louvain clustering

The course uses the first 40 PCs, Seurat's 20-neighbor default, the shared
nearest-neighbor graph, Louvain, and resolution 0.8. BioLang uses those same
analysis choices. At this dataset size a compatible GPU scans distances in
bounded batches and returns the top neighbors without allocating an all-pairs
matrix. The CPU fallback uses a deterministic random-projection forest.

```biolang
println("SNN: 20 neighbors over 40 corrected PCs")
let graph = sc.neighbors(corrected, 20, true, 40)
println("Louvain: resolution 0.8")
let clustered = sc.cluster_louvain(graph, 20, 0.8)
let cluster_count = clustered.clusters |> unique |> len
println("clusters: " + str(cluster_count) +
    " (historical HBC HTML: 17; Seurat 5.5.1 oracle: 19)")
```

## 6. UMAP

UMAP coordinates are not compared point-for-point: rotations, reflections, and
stochastic optimization change coordinates without changing neighborhoods.

```biolang
println("UMAP: 40 corrected PCs, 30 neighbors, seed 123456")
let embedded = sc.run_umap(clustered, 40, 30, 0.3, 123456)
write_text("validation-results/hbc-biolang/umap.svg", sc.plot_embedding(embedded, embedded.clusters,
    "HBC PBMC data — BioLang clusters"))
```

## 7. RNA-scale marker discovery

HBC explicitly returns to log-normalized RNA values for marker testing. The
cluster labels come from the integrated analysis, while fold changes and
Wilcoxon tests below use the uncorrected RNA expression scale.

```biolang
println("RNA log-normalization for marker testing")
let marker_ready = sc.normalize(embedded, 10000.0)
let markers = sc.find_all_markers(marker_ready, 0.1, 0.25, true)
println("positive marker rows: " + str(len(markers)))
write_csv(table(markers), "validation-results/hbc-biolang/markers.csv")
```

## 8. Write the validation manifest

Cluster numbers are arbitrary labels, so external validation joins cells by
sample plus original barcode and computes permutation-invariant agreement.

```biolang
let rows = range(0, embedded.n_cells) |> map(|i| {
    let xy = embedded.umap[i]
    {
        sample: if embedded.batch_ids[i] == 0 { "ctrl" } else { "stim" },
        barcode: embedded.barcodes[i],
        cluster: embedded.clusters[i],
        umap_1: xy[0],
        umap_2: xy[1]
    }
})
write_csv(table(rows), "validation-results/hbc-biolang/cells.csv")
let pc = matrix(embedded.pca_scores)
write_csv(table({
    sample: embedded.batch_ids |> map(|batch| if batch == 0 { "ctrl" } else { "stim" }),
    barcode: embedded.barcodes,
    pc_1: mat_col(pc, 0), pc_2: mat_col(pc, 1), pc_3: mat_col(pc, 2),
    pc_4: mat_col(pc, 3), pc_5: mat_col(pc, 4), pc_6: mat_col(pc, 5),
    pc_7: mat_col(pc, 6), pc_8: mat_col(pc, 7), pc_9: mat_col(pc, 8),
    pc_10: mat_col(pc, 9), pc_11: mat_col(pc, 10), pc_12: mat_col(pc, 11),
    pc_13: mat_col(pc, 12), pc_14: mat_col(pc, 13), pc_15: mat_col(pc, 14),
    pc_16: mat_col(pc, 15), pc_17: mat_col(pc, 16), pc_18: mat_col(pc, 17),
    pc_19: mat_col(pc, 18), pc_20: mat_col(pc, 19), pc_21: mat_col(pc, 20),
    pc_22: mat_col(pc, 21), pc_23: mat_col(pc, 22), pc_24: mat_col(pc, 23),
    pc_25: mat_col(pc, 24), pc_26: mat_col(pc, 25), pc_27: mat_col(pc, 26),
    pc_28: mat_col(pc, 27), pc_29: mat_col(pc, 28), pc_30: mat_col(pc, 29),
    pc_31: mat_col(pc, 30), pc_32: mat_col(pc, 31), pc_33: mat_col(pc, 32),
    pc_34: mat_col(pc, 33), pc_35: mat_col(pc, 34), pc_36: mat_col(pc, 35),
    pc_37: mat_col(pc, 36), pc_38: mat_col(pc, 37), pc_39: mat_col(pc, 38),
    pc_40: mat_col(pc, 39)
}), "validation-results/hbc-biolang/pcs.csv")
write_csv(table([{
    cells: embedded.n_cells,
    genes: embedded.n_genes,
    variable_genes: len(embedded.integrated_features),
    pcs: 40,
    neighbors: 20,
    resolution: 0.8,
    clusters: cluster_count,
    compute_backend: anchors.compute_backend
}]), "validation-results/hbc-biolang/summary.csv")
println("validation manifests written")
```

## Validation contract

The future book may claim exact agreement only for deterministic preprocessing
checkpoints. For stochastic or independently implemented methods it must report
the measured comparison: variable-feature overlap, neighborhood overlap,
adjusted Rand index after joining the same cells, cluster-count difference, and
canonical marker recovery. A cluster count alone is never sufficient evidence.
